{
  "teamId": 140,
  "year": 2022,
  "narrative": "The 2022 Texas Rangers entered the season carrying cautious optimism — a pair of blockbuster free-agent signings in Corey Seager and Marcus Semien promised a new era of contention in Arlington. But the roster around those stars was thin, and the opening month made that painfully clear. Texas stumbled to a 7-14 April, with the bullpen already showing cracks and the lineup unable to string enough consistent production together to stay competitive.\n\nMay offered a genuine reprieve. The Rangers surged to 17-10, their best month of the season, fueled by clutch hits from unlikely contributors like Brad Miller and Andy Ibáñez, and timely home runs from Nathaniel Lowe. It was a stretch that briefly made believers out of a fanbase hungry for relevance, and for a moment, the team's investment in its star middle infield seemed to be paying dividends. June began with a few more bright spots — Ezequiel Duran's electric debut triple, Adolis García continuing to play with flair — but the bullpen's volatility, particularly from Joe Barlow, began costing the Rangers games they had no business losing.\n\nJuly brought a rollercoaster identity: Seager and Lowe delivered back-to-back fireworks on the Fourth of July weekend, but the same Barlow who kept surrendering leads ensured those highs never lasted. The Rangers went 10-17 in July, and by August the season had curdled into something the front office could only hope to salvage for future lessons. The pitching staff — from starters like Martín Pérez to relievers like Brock Burke and Jonathan Hernández — absorbed beatings from Houston, Colorado, and Boston in sequence.\n\nSeptember was a quiet, disappointing fade. A 68-94 finish confirmed what the numbers had whispered all year: Texas was not yet a contender, only a team with two star players and a roster still being assembled. The season's silver lining was what it revealed — Lowe's emergence as a core piece, García's power and energy, Duran's promise — building blocks for the future, even as 2022 itself slipped away.",
